VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W2
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Scope: delta since W2.1 (aria-valuetext added to portfolio-settings.html's
canonical range, quick-adjust.html's mirror range, synced via
quick-adjust-scripts.html's syncQuickAdjustKey). Verified on a `cp -a` copy
of /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2, branch fix/phase-visibility.

BASELINE (green):
- `go build ./...` -> exit 0
- `go vet ./...` -> exit 0
- `go test ./...` -> all packages ok, no FAIL
- `make css-verify` -> "tailwind.css is up to date"

REGRESSION SWEEP of attempt-1 attack surfaces (all still hold):
- Single name-carrier invariant intact: `grep -rn 'name="monthly_living_expenses"' web/templates/`
  returns exactly one hit, the hidden input (portfolio-settings.html:61).
  The visible range and the quick-adjust mirror (quick-adjust.html:106) both
  carry no `name` attribute.
- hx-trigger on the settings form is byte-identical to attempt 1
  (`change delay:500ms, input delay:500ms from:find input[type=number]`) —
  the range/hidden/mirror controls are still non-number types, so the
  restricted number-input trigger still cannot fire mid-drag.
- Cents/off-grid round-trip: `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/...` (which
  includes living_expenses_snap_trap_test.go) still green.

NEW DEFECT — aria-valuetext format drift (FAIL, WCAG 4.1.2, directly
contradicts the worker's own stated invariant):

The fix adds two independent code paths that both compute aria-valuetext
from the *same* underlying exact value, but with two different rounding
rules that visibly disagree on ordinary inputs:

1. Server-side initial render (portfolio-settings.html:73, and
   quick-adjust.html:106):
     aria-valuetext="${{formatNumber .Settings.MonthlyLivingExpenses}}"
   `formatNumber` (internal/templates/render.go:546) computes
   `fmt.Sprintf("%.0f", v)`, which Go implements with round-half-to-even.

2. JS re-sync (quick-adjust-scripts.html, syncQuickAdjustKey, key ==
   'monthly_living_expenses'):
     const exactValueText = '$' + Math.round(Number(canonicalControl.value) || 0).toLocaleString();
   `Math.round` rounds half-away-from-zero (ties round up), not
   half-to-even.

For any settings value whose cents are exactly .50 — which is not a
contrived edge case: handlers.go:1206 computes
`settings.MonthlyLivingExpenses = totalExpenses / months` for the
Sync-from-Dashboard path with no rounding, and totalExpenses/months lands on
an exact .50 for perfectly ordinary inputs (e.g. $44,319.00 / 6 = 7386.50)
— the two paths disagree by $1:

  Reproduced directly against the code in this branch:
    $ go run a Go snippet: fmt.Sprintf("%.0f", 7386.50) -> "7386"
    $ node -e 'console.log(Math.round(7386.50))'        -> 7387

  So for MonthlyLivingExpenses == 7386.50:
    - Initial paint (server, formatNumber):        aria-valuetext="$7,386"
    - Immediately after DOMContentLoaded fires:
      `syncAllQuickAdjustControls()` (quick-adjust-scripts.html:202-205)
      unconditionally calls `syncQuickAdjustKey('monthly_living_expenses')`
      for the canonical hidden control on every page load — with NO drag,
      NO OOB swap, nothing but the page finishing loading — which
      overwrites the same attribute to aria-valuetext="$7,387".
    - The same overwrite reoccurs on every `/whatif/sync` OOB swap
      (htmx:oobAfterSwap -> scheduleQuickAdjustSync -> syncAllQuickAdjustControls),
      i.e. the server re-renders "$7,386" into the OOB fragment and the JS
      handler immediately flips it back to "$7,387".
    - The visible display span (formatQuickAdjustDisplay, 'currency' case,
      unchanged pre-existing code) renders `'$' + Number(value).toLocaleString()`
      with no rounding at all: "$7,386.5" — a third, still different string.

This directly falsifies the worker's own claim in the code comments: "kept
in sync... so it can never drift" and "aria-valuetext always carries the
exact saved/dragged dollar figure" — it demonstrably drifts by $1 between
the two code paths that are supposed to agree, on a value the feature's own
comments elsewhere call out as the realistic case ("e.g. 7386, from 'Sync
from Dashboard'"), and a screen-reader user tabbing into the control
immediately after page load will hear the JS-computed figure, not the
server one that briefly existed pre-DOMContentLoaded — so the claim that
"the very-first paint... is also correct for AT" does not hold either: for
a .50-cent value the first paint is already about to be overwritten to a
different number within the same event loop turn.

This is a genuine desync introduced by attempt 2's own new code (the JS
Math.round expression is new in this diff; git diff master confirms the
`if (key === 'monthly_living_expenses')` block including the aria-valuetext
lines did not exist before this attempt), not a pre-existing, already-
excused gap. It sits squarely in the attack surface this checker was asked
to hunt: "server renders the initial aria-valuetext with one formatter, JS
updates it with another — can they disagree."

No test in living_expenses_phase_note_test.go, living_expenses_snap_trap_test.go,
or elsewhere in the manifest exercises a .50-cent MonthlyLivingExpenses value
through either formatter, so nothing in the existing suite catches this.

Ruling: FAIL. Fix requires the JS and Go rounding rules to agree (e.g. have
the JS path also round-half-to-even, or have both round using the same
explicit rule, or avoid rounding to whole dollars at all on the JS side —
e.g. render the exact figure with cents like the display formatter would if
it were fixed, or share one formatting convention end-to-end) before this
can be reverified.
